Z||000000||DaveL||01-05-2001||03:53 PM||||I hate the Haynes manual. Just put new antifreeze in my 87 2.5, and got it but not without the usual glitch of some sort. I parked nose down on the slpe in the drive, and emptied all old fluid. Left the radiator cap off and filled it from the rear until the radiator was full, put the radiator cap on, then filled the thermostat housing up. With thermostat out, turned the thermostat cover only to the first click. Started it up and let it idle, while watching the guage. After a few minutes, it shot up, so I took off the thermostat cap and a small gush of fluid shot out (used a large rag fortunately). I filled it the rest of the way up through the thermostat opening, and so far no trouble. Should that have happened?||||reg||1
Z||000001||TheFiero64||01-06-2001||10:24 AM||||There was probably an air pocket. It should be ok now. Just check the fluid after a few runs<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-07-2001||05:40 PM||||Whenever the fiero is drained the heater and it's hoses will trap considerable amounts of air in them. This is why I worked out the filling procedure in my cave.<P>Since your heater wasn't drained then only the lines should have been holding air.<P>Either way what happened is pretty close to normal. As long as you don't keep getting more air in there then the system should be fine. <P>If you keep getting air in it then you have a problem someplace you need to fix.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaveL||01-09-2001||03:03 PM||||Oh, no, I forgot to flush the heater! Back to the classroom for me...||||reg||1
